---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";

const question = "Can dry lining be used in a loft conversion?";
const answer = `Yes — dry lining is the standard finish for loft conversions in Maidstone. Plasterboard is fixed either directly to the rafters (where headroom permits) or to a timber or metal stud frame between them.

Maidstone loft conversions must meet:

- Building Regulations Part B (Fire Safety) — 30-minute fire resistance to escape routes, fire-rated plasterboard where required
- Part E (Sound) — 40 dB Rw between converted and unconverted space
- Part L (Thermal) — U-value 0.18 W/m²K or better for the pitched roof, usually achieved with 100-150mm mineral wool between rafters plus a thermal liner
- Part C (Damp) — vapour control layer on the warm side of the insulation

We install insulated plasterboard to meet Part L, vapour control layers to prevent condensation, and fire-rated plasterboard where Part B requires. Building Control sign-off is handled by Maidstone Borough Council Building Control on your behalf.`;
const description = `Yes — dry lining is the standard finish for loft conversions in Maidstone. Plasterboard is fixed either directly to the rafters (where headroom permits) or to a t`;
const canonical = "/drylining-faq/can-dry-lining-be-used-in-a-loft-conversion/";

