      </header>

      <section style="padding: 18px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); margin-bottom: 22px;">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 1rem; font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text);">Yes — dry lining is the standard finish for loft conversions in Maidstone. Plasterboard is fixed either directly to the rafters (where headroom permits) or to a timber or metal stud frame between them.</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 1rem; font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text);">Maidstone loft conversions must meet:</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 1rem; font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text);">- Building Regulations Part B (Fire Safety) — 30-minute fire resistance to escape routes, fire-rated plasterboard where required
- Part E (Sound) — 40 dB Rw between converted and unconverted space
- Part L (Thermal) — U-value 0.18 W/m²K or better for the pitched roof, usually achieved with 100-150mm mineral wool between rafters plus a thermal liner
- Part C (Damp) — vapour control layer on the warm side of the insulation</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 1rem; font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text);">We install insulated plasterboard to meet Part L, vapour control layers to prevent condensation, and fire-rated plasterboard where Part B requires. Building Control sign-off is handled by Maidstone Borough Council Building Control on your behalf.</p>
